  ],
  "text": "For most of spaceflight history, every rocket was used exactly once. Imagine scrapping an aircraft after one flight and building a new one for the journey home. Nobody was being wasteful. Landing the thing was simply thought impossible. A booster comes back empty, moving fast, and far too light to throttle down gently. So you relight the engine at exactly the right moment, and only briefly. Too early and you run out of fuel. Too late and you simply arrive at the ground. Grid fins steer it on the way down, and the legs unfold in the last few seconds. And it lands on a boat, because the ocean is where the trajectory was already heading. Cost per kilogram to orbit fell by roughly a factor of twenty inside a decade. Cheap launch is upstream of everything. Every plan beyond low orbit was waiting on this one number. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
